PUBLIC RESPONSE: C

The circumflex accent (ˆ) in French has a range of historically attested orthographic functions, all of which can be documented from the 16th–19th centuries onward. These include:
- marking a contrast in vowel quality (open vs. close timbre), especially â [ɑ] vs. a [a] (A);
- disambiguating homophones (dû vs. du, sûr vs. sur) (B);
- indicating earlier vowel length (D);
- signalling the reduction of an earlier diphthong or of two vowels in hiatus (âge < aage, rôle < roole) (G, I);
- showing the site of a lost sibilant (forêt < forest, hôpital < hospital) (H);
- and occasional analogical or “decorative” additions intended to give a word a more learned appearance (F).

Option C, however, does not correspond to any documented use. While ô frequently represents [o] and many such words are ultimately of Latin origin, the circumflex itself is never employed specifically to signal “a vowel pronounced [o] because the word comes from Classical Latin.” The pronunciation and spelling are instead explained by other historical processes (loss of s, compensatory lengthening, etc.). Consequently C is the function that has never been attested.

